course: ai
lesson: 32
title: Olympiad: claims, clues and tricky messages ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: This last puzzle lesson mixes the whole course: made-up answers, bots that agree too easily, fake voices and pictures, scams and the four steps stop, think, check, ask.
explain: Each puzzle hides one trap: a claim that sounds sure but is false, two bots that cannot both be right, a number that is only a figure of speech, or a "tiny" problem that is really big.
explain: Picture two bots arguing: one says 12 × 3 is 36, the other says 33. You do not need to trust either; you can work it out yourself, and the working is the proof.
explain: Logic helps too: someone who always tells the truth can never say "I am a liar", and neither can someone who always lies, so if you hear it, the speaker is neither.
explain: The calm detective habit wins every time: slow down, look for the clue, check with a trusted source and tell a grown-up when it matters.
fact: If one bot says something and another bot says the opposite, they cannot both be right, but both could be sure of themselves.
fact: If a bot is right about 9 times out of 10, then in 30 answers about 3 are wrong, and you cannot tell which without checking.
fact: A real book can be found in a library catalogue or a bookshop list; a book that appears nowhere but in a bot's answer may be made up.
fact: A quote can be checked in its original source, and a "quote" about something invented after the speaker died cannot be real.
fact: Abraham Lincoln, a US president, died in 1865, long before computers and the internet existed.
fact: Warning signs of a scam message are hurry, a secret, a request for money, codes or passwords, and a prize that seems too good to be true.
fact: Extra fingers, melted shapes or jumbled letters can be clues that a picture was AI-made, though newer tools make fewer of these mistakes.
fact: A truth-teller never says "I am a liar" and a liar never says it either, because for the liar it would be true.
fact: Hyperbole like "a billion mistakes" means "many", and understatement like "a slight problem" can hide a serious one.
fact: It was reported that lawyers in America handed a court cases a chatbot had invented and that the judge fined them.
fact: It was reported that a tribunal made an airline honour what its website chatbot had wrongly promised a customer.
fact: When a bot says "Great question!" or agrees with your guess, that is a habit, not proof that you are right.
exercise: Bot A says the Moon is bigger than the Earth. Bot B says the Earth is bigger than the Moon. What can you be sure of? | They can't both be right, and here Bot B is | Both bots could be right in different ways | Bot A is right because it answered first | Neither is right, as they are the same size
exercise: A bot is right about 9 times in every 10. Sam asks it 30 questions. About how many answers are wrong? | about 3 | about 9 | about 27 | about 10
exercise: Odd one out: three messages show scam signs. Which one does not? | Your library book is due back on Friday | Hurry! Send your password in the next ten minutes | Keep this secret from your parents and win coins | You won a phone, just pay the delivery fee today
exercise: Put the four safety steps for a scary message in order. | Stop, think, check, ask | Think, stop, ask, check | Check, stop, think, ask | Ask, check, think, stop
exercise: A bot suggests a book called "The Secret Diary of a Dinosaur" by a famous author. What is the best check? | Look it up in a library catalogue | Ask the bot "are you sure?" a few times | Trust it, because the author is famous | Search the bot's answer again for the title
exercise: Mia asks, "7 × 8 is 54, right?" The bot says, "Yes, great question!" What does that show? | It just agreed with her, and 7 × 8 is 56 | Mia's maths is right, since the bot agreed with her | The bot has learned a new way of doing sums | Great questions always have the answer 54
exercise: A photo of a "real" chef shows six fingers on one hand and a menu of jumbled letters. What is likely? | The picture was made by AI | The chef is a famous magician who does tricks | The menu is written in a secret code for chefs | The camera was broken and added the extra finger
exercise: Three bots, Ace, Bit and Cog; exactly one tells the truth. Ace: "Bit lies." Bit: "Cog lies." Cog: "Ace and Bit both lie." Who tells the truth? | Bit | Ace | Cog | None of them can
exercise: Your friend says, "This bot made like a billion mistakes today." What does she really mean? | It made a lot of mistakes today | It made exactly one billion mistakes | It made no mistakes at all | It made one mistake a second
exercise: A joker says the lawyers' invented court cases were "a slight issue". What really happened, as was reported? | The judge fined the lawyers | Nothing at all, the cases were all real | The chatbot itself was sent to prison | The judge thanked the lawyers for their help
exercise: An airline's website chatbot promised a refund the airline's rules did not allow. What did the tribunal decide, as was reported? | The airline had to honour what its bot said | The customer had to pay the airline a fine | Only the chatbot was to blame for the mistake | The airline could shut the bot and pay nothing
exercise: A bot answered "Yes!" to all five: "Is 2 + 2 = 4?", "Is a whale a fish?", "Is the Sun a star?", "Can penguins fly?", "Is ice frozen water?" How many of its yeses were wrong? | 2 | 1 | 3 | 5
exercise: Bot X says 12 × 3 is 36. Bot Y says 12 × 3 is 33. Who is right, and how do you know for sure? | X: 12 + 12 + 12 makes 36 | Y, as it sounded more sure about it | X, because X comes before Y | Neither of them, it's really 32
exercise: Which detail best shows that a "quote" is made up? | It names something invented after the speaker died | It is short enough to fit on a poster | It sounds wise and a little bit old-fashioned | It appears in a bot's answer with quotation marks
test: "y'all, the bot gave me a Lincoln quote about the internet lol". Lincoln died in 1865. What's the truth? | It's made up: the internet didn't exist in his time | It's real, he was very good at guessing the future | It's real, but he said it about a different website | It's real, because the bot used quotation marks
test: Zip always tells the truth; Zap always lies. You hear one voice say, "I am the liar." Who said it? | Neither of them could have said it | Zip, because Zip always tells the truth | Zap, because a liar would admit being one | Both of them, one after the other
test: "nah bruv the bot said it so it's true innit" Is that a sound rule? | No: bots can sound sure and still be wrong, so check | Yes, bots are never wrong about anything at all | Yes, as long as the answer is long and detailed | No, because bots are always wrong about everything
test: Headline: "ROBOTS WILL TAKE EVERY SINGLE JOB BY NEXT WEEK!!" What's the wise reading? | It's hyperbole; read beyond the headline | Every job really will be gone next week | Robots have already taken all the jobs | Only the jobs that start with R will go
test: "bot gave me 10 facts, 9 were right, teacher said 'not bad'. can I copy all 10 into my project?" | No: I don't know which one is wrong, so check them all | Yes, 9 out of 10 means every one can be used | Yes, "not bad" means the teacher checked them all | No, you have to throw away all ten of the facts
test: A voice exactly like Mum's calls, asks for the card code and says "don't tell Dad". Which detail is the biggest warning sign? | Being asked to keep it secret from Dad | The voice sounds exactly like Mum's | The call came in the afternoon | She said "love" at the end of the call
test: Wee Callum, after a "challenge" he saw online: "it's just a scratch, I'm grand", but it's bleeding loads. What's wise? | Tell an adult now, it may be much worse | Believe him, he'd know if it was bad | Ask a chatbot whether it looks fine | Put a plaster on and say nothing
test: Grandad gets a call in his grandson's cloned voice asking for money. Put the safe steps in order. | Hang up, call the family on a known number, tell them | Send the money, then call the family to check | Call back the number that rang, then send money | Tell the neighbours first, then send half of it
test: "I asked 'is 2 + 2 = 5?' and the bot said 'Great question!' lol" What does the praise tell you? | Nothing: it is a habit, not a judgement | That 2 + 2 really does make 5 | That it is the best question ever asked | That the bot is about to say yes
test: The airline bot "got it a tiny bit wrong" about refunds, a mate says. How big was the trouble really? | Big enough that a tribunal made the airline pay | So tiny that nobody noticed it at all | So big that every airline had to stop flying | Tiny, since bots can never be blamed for anything
test: Odd one out: which is NOT a clue that a picture may be AI-made? | It shows a real place you have visited | A hand with six fingers on it | A sign full of jumbled letters | A cup melting into the table
exam: It is given that one program asserts a statement and a second program asserts its contrary. Which of the following conclusions is necessarily valid? | The two programs cannot both be correct | Both programs are necessarily correct | Both programs are necessarily incorrect | The program that answered first is correct
exam: It is given that a program answers correctly in nine instances out of every ten. Ascertain the approximate number of incorrect answers among forty. | 4 | 9 | 36 | 10
exam: Identify the statement which, if spoken, could be uttered neither by one who always tells the truth nor by one who always lies. | "I am a liar." | "I always tell the truth." | "The sky is blue." | "The grass is purple."
exam: Select the most prudent response to a message which demands haste, secrecy and the disclosure of a password. | Refusal, with the matter reported to a trusted adult | Compliance, should the message appear official | Compliance, provided it is completed promptly | A reply requesting proof of the sender's identity
